1. The Backflip Test: latent intelligence and expressed capability

Claim

An internal intuition may be genuine without yet being communicable, but latent potential is not the same as instantiated capability.

“I understand it but cannot explain it” can mean:

• a real nonverbal or procedural model lacks a suitable output interface;
• the person can recognize the answer but cannot reconstruct it;
• familiarity or felt profundity is being mistaken for understanding;
• the claim is protecting a desired identity from missing performance.

The backflip analogy must be used carefully. A backflip is defined by physical execution, whereas understanding may leave tracks through multiple channels: explanation, prediction, recognition, correction, transfer, construction, or action.

Canonical test

> If alleged knowledge produces no reliable delta through any available channel, calling it knowledge is presently unjustified.

Operational definition

> Understanding is the ability to transform a representation without destroying what makes it true.

Track test

```pseudo
function test_understanding(model):
    channels = [explain, predict, recognize, correct, transfer, build, act]
    tracks = evaluate_reliable_deltas(model, channels)
    if tracks.is_empty():
        return "unsubstantiated latent claim"
    if tracks.exist_but_do_not_transfer():
        return "local/tacit competence"
    return "operational understanding, confidence proportional to tracks"
```

Failure mode

Do not make verbal fluency sovereign. Do not make unverifiable inward certainty sovereign either.

