4. Light, shadow, darkness, and abjection

Shadow simply is

Shadow is not a moral category. It is a relationship among light, obstruction, and position. Darkness can carry rest, incubation, privacy, mystery, recovery, concealment, predation, or harm. Its morality is not intrinsic.

Brighter light can cast sharper shadows when an obstruction remains. Illumination is therefore not identical to elimination.

Differentiation operator

Light can differentiate:

• mystery from deception;
• privacy from secrecy;
• anger from cruelty;
• appetite from predation;
• rest from stagnation;
• shadow from hidden harm.

What can be integrated becomes depth, contrast, boundary, warning, or rest. Harmful conduct must still be constrained.

Abjection

Abjection is the expulsion of something because it threatens the boundary between self and not-self, order and disorder, or human and inhuman.

> Disgust says, “I dislike that.” Abjection says, “That must be expelled because its existence destabilizes who I believe I am.”

In Pantheon terms, abjection is an attempted deletion of the shadow to avoid revising the self-model. Integration recognizes and governs; abjection alienates and projects.

Reconciliation condition

> The light is not here to erase your existence. It is here to end your need to hide.

The reciprocal warning is that illumination without mercy can become interrogation.

