10. Games beneath the throne

The throne game is highly legible: titles, money, territory, votes, followers, command. Its cards are standardized and widely recognized, making them easy to play—and easy to corrupt.

Visibility is not sovereignty. Beneath the throne are substrate games:

• legitimacy: whether commands work without constant force;
• attention: what is noticed and therefore treated as real;
• trust: which relationships coordinate under uncertainty;
• meaning: what sacrifices people willingly bear;
• succession: what survives the current player;
• identity: which moves people can imagine themselves making;
• time: who can wait while others exhaust themselves.

Popular cards can be corrupted by inflation, spoofed status, poisoned information, captured advisors, gamed metrics, or redefined language. An opponent need not defeat the hand if they can change what the cards mean.

Causal-reach test

Hidden does not automatically mean profound. An obscure game may simply be irrelevant.

> Which game determines whether the mountain still matters, and who is writing its rules?

The throne is an interface. Its deeper power depends upon the substrates it can still command.

