12. Mission Before Metric / Anti-Manufactured Scarcity

Optimization is a servant. When the system sacrifices its purpose to improve its own measurements, optimization becomes the product.

Manufactured scarcity often begins as a rational control—preserving quality, preventing overload, allocating demand—then becomes profitable or institutionally convenient. The system protects the bottleneck it was meant to solve.

Objective correction

Reject:

[
\max(\text{measured output})
]

Prefer:

[
\max(\text{human value})
\quad\text{subject to resilience, slack, dignity, and real constraints}
]

Slack appears wasteful to a narrow optimizer, yet hosts recovery, play, generosity, serendipity, and adaptation. A perfectly optimized organism may be one regime change away from death.

Guardrail

Scarcity cannot be abolished; abundance in one domain often moves scarcity elsewhere. Information abundance produces attention scarcity. Option abundance can produce decision scarcity.

> Stop manufacturing unnecessary scarcity while preserving constraints that generate safety, meaning, quality, renewal, or consent.

Never let the metric eat the mission.

