The portable part of laissez-faire in intimate life is decentralized adult choice: free association and mutual consent.

The “sexual marketplace” metaphor can illuminate competition, scarcity, signaling, and preference. It fails when it converts persons into inventory, rejection into theft, or desire into entitlement.

[ \text{legitimate intimate freedom}

\text{free association}
+\text{informed consent}
+\text{honesty}
+\text{reciprocity}
+\text{responsibility for consequences}
]

Consent remains revocable. Access cannot be purchased as a claim on another person. Coercion, deception, age, dependency, disease risk, pregnancy, and harm to families prevent intimacy from behaving like a simple commodity market.

Reciprocal surveillance warning

Crowdsourced sexual-history or dating-warning systems may emerge symmetrically across genders, but symmetry does not remove risks: defamation, false reports, harassment, permanent identity capture, privacy violations, and abjection. Accountability mechanisms should protect people without turning their histories into immutable caste scores.

